00:02:37.309 --> 00:02:39.100
DAVE ELLIOTT: So size,
growth, scalability,

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:43.120
one of the kind of defining
features of all types of media.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:43.750
OK, great.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:44.250
Great.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.859
So what else are you
seeing with media?

00:02:46.859 --> 00:02:47.400
It's growing.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.275
It's growing rapidly.

00:02:48.275 --> 00:02:51.365
So how does that translate
into a requirement for storage?

00:02:51.365 --> 00:02:52.990
JEFF KEMBER: Well,
we made a transition

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:54.890
from standard-def to
high-def many years ago,

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:57.020
and everything is
pretty much 1080p now.

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.799
And moving forward,
though, 4k has caught

00:02:59.799 --> 00:03:01.090
on in the last couple of years.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:02.660
We're seeing that
it's not a fad.

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:04.740
It's an actual
growth trend for us.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.312
Customers are paying
for 4k content.

00:03:06.312 --> 00:03:07.520
DAVE ELLIOTT: It's not a fad.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:08.020
All right.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.060
JEFF KEMBER: Not a fad.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.810
Customers are also
interested in HDR.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:14.404
