shadow detail and brightness.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:25.650
The image looks quite a bit more
lifelike than standard imagery

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:26.200
does today.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.260
DAVE ELLIOTT: So these consume
enormous amounts of storage,

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:28.630
I assume.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:29.270
JEFF KEMBER: Absolutely.

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:30.853
When you go up in
resolution, and then

00:03:30.853 --> 00:03:32.830
when you go up in color
depth, you very quickly

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:34.870
grow the size of the
file significantly.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:37.125
And that is yet
another type of flavor

00:03:37.125 --> 00:03:38.500
that you need to
make when you're

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.490
producing a video streaming
archive, for instance.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:41.365
DAVE ELLIOTT: OK, OK.

00:03:41.365 --> 00:03:42.900
So growth, huge issue.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.975
So I assume scalability, then,
is a significant requirement

00:03:46.975 --> 00:03:51.240
when we're talking
about designing storage

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.377
architectures for media.

00:03:52.377 --> 00:03:53.460
So what else is important?

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:54.389
Protection?

00:03:54.389 --> 00:03:55.180
JEFF KEMBER: Right.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:56.350
Security is key.

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:58.650
You need to ensure that
only you have access

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.150
to the data, and
only the people you

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:03.130
grant access to have access
to the specific bits of data

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:03.800
you want.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.970
An example would be if you have
a massive data archive that

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.272
is proprietary to
your company, you

00:04:09.272 --> 00:04:10.730
want to ensure that
you can provide

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:13.270
just distribution
for elements of that

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.040
and have great controls
over who gets at that data

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:17.890
and, of course,
no one else does.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:19.230
DAVE ELLIOTT: So access
control, encryption,

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:20.855
and things like that
are important when

00:04:20.855 --> 00:04:23.380
you're storing these very,
very valuable assets.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:24.570
JEFF KEMBER: Yes.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.530
DAVE ELLIOTT: What about just,
generally, losing the assets?

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:28.510
What about durability?

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:28.860
JEFF KEMBER: Right.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:31.010
It becomes very expensive
to have multiple copies,

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.555
and manage multiple copies, from
a version syncing standpoint

00:04:33.555 --> 00:04:34.140
and such.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:38.220
So having a highly durable,
single point, source is lovely.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:41.150
Paying someone else to
manage that storage for you?

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:41.954
Extremely valuable.

00:04:41.954 --> 00:04:43.370
When you do it
on-prem, you end up

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.280
having high
availability storage.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:46.560
You have cooler storage.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.910
You have mountains of tapes
that are stored, both on-prem

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:51.630
and in cold storage off.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:53.030
DAVE ELLIOTT: And I imagine
this is how a lot of companies

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:53.530
do it today.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:56.610
So high durability
is a key requirement.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:59.071
And companies today likely
have multiple replicas in order

00:04:59.071 --> 00:05:00.570
to meet that
durability requirement.

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:01.920
JEFF KEMBER: They're often
contract contractually

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.437
required to have three,
depending on the type of work.

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:06.020
DAVE ELLIOTT: What
about availability?

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:07.210
How important is that?

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:08.720
JEFF KEMBER: Yeah,
availability is critical.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.470
On the project that
you may be working on,

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:14.310
having the media available
to you randomly is key.

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:16.450
If it takes you hours to
be able to pull the data

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:19.810
back, or even days, if you have
to online the data from tape,

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:21.810
and the tape may
not be on-premises,

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:25.230
it takes hours to get it, or
up to potentially three days,

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:28.277
to get the data back overnight,
online it, look at it,

00:05:28.277 --> 00:05:30.110
and realize you didn't
restore enough of it,

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.110
or you restored too much
of it, and therefore it

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:33.620
took too long to restore.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:35.280
And there's often
dependencies that

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.950
are found in that restored data
that then point to other data

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:39.741
that you, then, have
to repeat the process.

00:05:39.741 --> 00:05:42.145
DAVE ELLIOTT: So on-demand
availability is key on that?

00:05:42.145 --> 00:05:43.020
JEFF KEMBER: Correct.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.170
And that was only
a local restore.

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:47.850
You're then in a scenario where,
if you have a global office

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:50.070
elsewhere and the data
is not local to them,

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:52.815
you have to work with the
time zone of that local office

00:05:52.815 --> 00:05:55.190
to get that data back, and
then ship it to where you are.

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:56.660
DAVE ELLIOTT: Or imagine
a consumer that's in India

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:58.490
and might want to
listen to a music file,

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.410
or someone who's
in Paris may want

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.970
to see an image, a
photograph, that is stored

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:05.230
in some rich media file asset.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.830
And so, the ability to
have that on-demand access

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:09.022
globally is important.

00:06:09.022 --> 00:06:09.730
JEFF KEMBER: Yes.

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:11.770
Consumers are definitely
focused on getting

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:13.580
things on-demand and globally.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:15.840
I mean, the web services
all provide that today.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:16.510
DAVE ELLIOTT: Great, great.

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:18.320
And then, I know you
also talk a little bit

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:20.620
about the importance,
from an availability

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.370
standpoint, of just generally
knowing what the heck you have.

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:23.900
JEFF KEMBER: Right.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:24.400
Yes.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:27.820
I've worked with customers
who have large vaults,

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:30.360
and I'm talking a cabinet
with hard drives in them,

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.716
and they'll be
handwritten post-it notes

00:06:32.716 --> 00:06:34.090
with an elastic
band around them,

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:36.450
and that's the limit of
the knowledge of the data.

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:37.890
And they said, yes, the
people who wrote those notes

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:40.950
aren't here anymore, but we've
passed it down over time.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.654
And that's unfortunate,
in the fact that

00:06:43.654 --> 00:06:44.820
how do you get at that data?

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.610
You literally have to
take that hard drive

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:49.030
and mount it in a machine that
has the correct interface,

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:50.620
and then it's hopefully
running the right OS

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:51.995
to be able to pull
that data off,

00:06:51.995 --> 00:06:53.550
and then you have
to sift through it.

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:56.710
That's a horribly expensive
task, in terms of human time.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:59.240
And so the question is whether
or not that data actually

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:00.140
gets read again.

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:02.520
So the value of that
data may be really high,

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:06.320
but the usefulness of it, the
value of it, though, to you,

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:09.320
the IP owner, is really low, in
the fact you can't get at it.

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:10.150
DAVE ELLIOTT: Because
the cost is high.

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:10.650
Got it.

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:12.370
So a rich metadata
model might be

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:13.990
something that
would be valuable,

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:17.490
so that you can describe these
media assets in a rich way,

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:21.600
and be able to have easy
access, and essentially better

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:22.700
availability to them?

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:23.440
JEFF KEMBER: Absolutely.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:24.565
DAVE ELLIOTT: Great, great.

00:07:24.565 --> 00:07:27.790
OK, so we've heard-- I guess
to summarize, what I've heard

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:29.232
is scalability is important.

00:07:29.232 --> 00:07:29.940
JEFF KEMBER: Yes.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:32.760
DAVE ELLIOTT: Media files
are growing incredibly fast.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.120
Flexibility, so you need
to be able to support

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:36.380
small files and large files.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:38.640
You need to be able to
self-describe the files,

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.530
or have the files described in
some way with rich metadata.

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:44.540
Security, protection,
durability,

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:46.830
and then, generally,
availability, are important.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:47.538
JEFF KEMBER: Yes.

00:07:47.538 --> 00:07:51.080
Yeah, the global availability
is really the exciting bit.

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:55.080
It's been challenging working in
the field with multiple offices

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:57.950
globally, and you're having to
rely on file synchronization,

00:07:57.950 --> 00:08:00.660
dedicated links,
multiple file systems

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:02.450
in each of the
disparate offices that,

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:04.277
then, require synchronization.

00:08:04.277 --> 00:08:05.360
There's a lot of overhead.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:08.620
There's multiple people required
to make sure that system works.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:10.110
With a central
global repository,

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:12.500
you can just have individuals
access the data they need.

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.590
For me, that's super
exciting from an availability

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:15.110
standpoint.

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:16.110
DAVE ELLIOTT: Fantastic.

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:16.650
Fantastic.

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:18.150
So now that we've
chatted some about

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:21.200
the interesting requirements
for storage, and storing media,

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.270
I'd like to touch on object
storage and cloud storage,

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:26.330
and how those fit many
of the requirements

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:27.560
that Jeff just mentioned.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:29.580
Then, I'll introduce
cloud storage,

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.510
and provide a brief
overview of how Google Cloud

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:34.539
Storage is different.

00:08:34.539 --> 00:08:36.630
So a blob store,
or an object store,

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:39.549
is a storage architecture
that manages data as objects,

00:08:39.549 --> 00:08:42.419
versus a storage architecture,
like a file system, which

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:44.260
manages data as
a file hierarchy,

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:46.710
or block storage, which
manages data as blocks

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:48.170
within sectors or tracks.

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:50.150
So two tenets of
object storage that

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:53.430
make them popular choice for
managing media and media assets

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:59.080
are first, they're a flat,
flexible, and scalable system.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:00.760
So traditional storage,
as I mentioned,

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:02.620
relies on file
system hierarchies,

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:05.650
which creates certain
upper limits on scalability

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:06.420
and performance.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.950
Object storage doesn't
have that limitation.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:11.350
As Jeff mentioned,
scalable performance

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:15.890
is a critical aspect of storing
and accessing media files.

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:17.940
Object storers also
provide flexibility

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:20.080
on the size of objects.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:21.520
The second thing
is object storers

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:24.000
provide a robust and
flexible metadata

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.040
layer, metadata capabilities.

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:28.350
As objects are
stored with the data,

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:31.530
they also have an address
and this robust and flexible

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:32.460
metadata.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.210
So the metadata
flexibility, compared

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:35.890
to other types of
storage, addresses

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:37.710
some of the
accessibility concerns

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:39.010
that Jeff mentioned earlier.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:42.880
More metadata means better
access and better control.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:44.760
So object storage isn't perfect.

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:46.110
It doesn't solve all problems.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:50.060
But it does bring simplicity and
flexibility to access and scale

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:52.230
for storing media files.

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:54.160
If you understand
object storage,

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:55.900
and why it's such
a popular choice,

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:57.316
I'd like to touch
a little bit now

00:09:57.316 --> 00:09:59.270
on object storage in the cloud.

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:03.760
Object storage in the cloud
adds additional benefits.

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:06.510
First, scale becomes global.

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:08.830
Jeff talked about scale
and how critical it was.

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:11.670
Scale becomes global
with global data centers.

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:13.580
So the larger the
cloud, the more capacity

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:16.070
you can use to store
your media assets that

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:18.375
may come during periods
of unpredictable growth.

00:10:18.375 --> 00:10:20.750
So Google manages one of the
largest clouds in the world,

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:24.380
and supports many, many,
many exabytes of storage.

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:28.650
Next, object storage in the
cloud can be more available.

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:30.490
So major cloud
providers provide data

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:31.770
centers all over the globe.

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:35.750
At Google, we have more than 100
Edge locations in 33 countries.

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:39.530
Data will stay online,
which is in stark contrast

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:41.040
to tape storage.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:44.660
Also, data can be accessed
with simple RESTful APIs.

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:49.000
Third, cloud storage should
be, could be, and likely is,

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:50.060
more durable.

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:52.270
A well constructed
cloud will deliver

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:55.881
highly redundant,
geographically diverse, storage.

00:10:55.881 --> 00:10:57.380
In the case of
Google Cloud Storage,

00:10:57.380 --> 00:11:01.660
we offer 11 9s durability across
all tiers of object storage.

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:04.310
And finally, a well
constructed cloud

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:06.224
should be even more secure.

00:11:06.224 --> 00:11:07.640
While a few years
ago, some people

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:10.160
believed cloud was less
secure because they didn't

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:12.130
manage security themselves.

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:16.010
Today, I think most people agree
that a well run cloud provider

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:19.570
benefits from having a very,
very large, and very focused,

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:20.770
team providing security.

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:24.220
At Google, we've got something
like more than 600 people

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:27.370
whose job it is to protect
our cloud infrastructure.

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:32.000
So overall, as with
any cloud service,

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:35.270
much of the heavy
lifting is designing

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:38.030
a durable, secure, scalable
cloud is outsourced.

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:41.110
So it lets you focus on
what you're doing best.

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:45.360
Would you say, Jeff, that those
key requirements-- scalability,

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:48.870
durability,
availability-- are some

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:54.380
of the key tenets that people
who are managing media assets

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:55.047
are looking for?

00:11:55.047 --> 00:11:56.046
JEFF KEMBER: Absolutely.

00:11:56.046 --> 00:11:58.690
The opportunity to be able
to get out any file any time

00:11:58.690 --> 00:12:00.100
is super valuable.

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:03.980
The example I can give would
be-- I was on a production

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:06.800
where there was a
directorial change,

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:08.195
and they needed
access to a file.

00:12:08.195 --> 00:12:09.570
DAVE ELLIOTT: That
never happens.

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:10.694
JEFF KEMBER: Never happens.

00:12:10.694 --> 00:12:12.820
Directors never
change their mind.

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:15.200
So in that context, we
needed to bring an asset

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:17.640
back online that had been
moved off of the fast storage,

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:18.410
essentially.

00:12:18.410 --> 00:12:21.932
And the version was available,
but we had to pull it back,

00:12:21.932 --> 00:12:23.890
and it was going to take
about a day and a half

00:12:23.890 --> 00:12:25.264
to get that data,
just because it

00:12:25.264 --> 00:12:26.570
was in another office and such.

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:30.900
So when I moved to another
firm, where 100% of the data

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:33.599
was online, in
terms of archival,

00:12:33.599 --> 00:12:35.140
we were able to
restore the data back

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:35.990
in just a couple of minutes.

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:37.330
You could just change
your shot directory,

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:39.204
go over to where you
needed to find the file,

00:12:39.204 --> 00:12:40.020
and pull it back.

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:42.880
And that emulates the
exact work use model

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:45.710
that we have for
Google Cloud Platform.

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:47.360
You can go and
pull the files back

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:49.460
in just a couple of
seconds, and you have access

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:50.950
to the entire data set.

00:12:50.950 --> 00:12:53.970
So the cost savings
on the several days

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:57.440
of people time and
schedule time-- wall clock,

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:58.797
as we call it-- was huge.

00:12:58.797 --> 00:12:59.630
DAVE ELLIOTT: Great.

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:00.130
Yeah.

00:13:00.130 --> 00:13:03.610
Yeah, I've heard types of those
stories over and over again.

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:06.000
It's the accessibility, the
availability, of the data,

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.375
and then what you
can do with it,

00:13:07.375 --> 00:13:09.852
and the time that you save in
trying to pull that data back

00:13:09.852 --> 00:13:12.060
is one of the key benefits
of having an object store,

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:13.360
and having a cloud object store.

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:13.850
JEFF KEMBER: Absolutely.

00:13:13.850 --> 00:13:15.683
When you look at the
total cost of ownership

00:13:15.683 --> 00:13:18.700
for on-premise storage,
in terms of people time

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:20.120
and the number of
days-- if you're

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:22.430
on a schedule of any
kind, and you can add up

00:13:22.430 --> 00:13:24.780
the salaries of all the
people, and add that

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:26.780
to your total cost of
overhead, and every day it

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:28.738
takes you longer to
complete it, because you're

00:13:28.738 --> 00:13:31.150
pulling storage, either your
offline or your online data,

00:13:31.150 --> 00:13:33.777
to try and find it,
that's all real cost.

00:13:33.777 --> 00:13:34.610
DAVE ELLIOTT: Great.

00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:35.530
Great.

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:38.360
So we've talked a little
bit about the requirements

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:43.370
for media, and managing
media files and media assets.

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:45.770
We then moved on to
talk a little about how

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:50.294
object store is a different
approach that really meets

00:13:50.294 --> 00:13:51.460
a lot of those requirements.

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:56.550
And then how cloud
object store is really

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:59.050
an even better approach.

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:01.130
So if you're managing
media assets,

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:06.860
or if you will in the future, I
guess you may ask the question,

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:08.720
you know, why Google?

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:11.460
And what's unique about Google?

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:13.950
So now I'd like to transition
into how Google, and Google

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:15.650
Cloud Storage, is different.

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:18.110
Google's had to cope with
massive amounts of data,

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:19.670
because of its grand scale.

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:21.630
Google services
are truly global,

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:24.015
generating exabytes
and exabytes of data.

00:14:24.015 --> 00:14:27.780
A secret to Google's continuing
success stems from the fact

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:30.930
that Google was able to operate
at this scale before anyone

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:34.320
else, and at price points order
of magnitudes less expensive

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:35.520
than competitors.

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:38.500
So as a result, there are
a few distinct advantages

00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:41.340
to our scale and our experience.

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:43.670
Google Cloud Storage lets
you store your media assets

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:46.720
on a globally available,
highly secure and efficient

00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:47.610
infrastructure.

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:50.040
Our object store is
differentiated in several ways.

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:53.146
First, we deliver massive,
massive scalability.

00:14:53.146 --> 00:14:54.020
Have I said that yet?

00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:54.490
JEFF KEMBER: You have.

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:55.570
DAVE ELLIOTT:
Massive scalability.

00:14:55.570 --> 00:14:56.930
Have I said lots and
lots of exabytes?

00:14:56.930 --> 00:14:57.290
JEFF KEMBER: Yeah.

00:14:57.290 --> 00:14:57.790
Many.

00:14:57.790 --> 00:14:58.499
How many?

00:14:58.499 --> 00:14:59.790
DAVE ELLIOTT: Many, many, many.

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:02.450
I can't tell you exactly, but
many, many, many, and growing

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:03.310
rapidly.

00:15:03.310 --> 00:15:06.710
We've as much capacity
as anyone needs.

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:08.950
One customer came
to us a while ago

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:11.270
and asked for several
hundred petabytes,

00:15:11.270 --> 00:15:14.780
because they couldn't procure
storage quickly enough,

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:17.820
and they said they wanted
to grow into maybe a couple

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:18.680
exabytes.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:22.160
Now, as a storage guy,
that's a lot of storage.

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:24.410
That is an unbelievable
amount of storage.

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:26.320
And we honestly didn't
have any concerns.

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:26.450
JEFF KEMBER: No.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:28.700
When they asked if they could
have an exabyte of data,

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:30.160
we could arrogantly
respond with,

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:31.620
how many exabytes
would you like?

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:32.745
DAVE ELLIOTT: Yes, exactly.

00:15:32.745 --> 00:15:34.190
Second, our global network.

00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:36.680
Our global network is
a huge differentiator.

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:39.040
We've got built-in EdgeCast
capabilities, where

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:42.490
we keep your data on our network
as long as we possibly can,

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:45.380
and we automatically store
replicas near where objects

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:46.994
are most frequently requested.

00:15:46.994 --> 00:15:48.910
So this means better
availability, and, hence,

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:51.180
a better user experience,
customer experience,

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:52.632
with your media assets.

00:15:52.632 --> 00:15:54.090
Our network, as I
mentioned before,

00:15:54.090 --> 00:15:58.530
has more than 100 Edge locations
in 33 countries for caching.

00:15:58.530 --> 00:15:59.825
You'll be tested on this.

00:15:59.825 --> 00:16:00.325
133.

00:16:00.325 --> 00:16:01.116
Keep those numbers.

00:16:01.116 --> 00:16:02.170
JEFF KEMBER: It's a quiz.

00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:03.753
DAVE ELLIOTT: And
we're the only cloud

00:16:03.753 --> 00:16:06.712
provider that actually has
laid cables across both oceans.

00:16:06.712 --> 00:16:08.420
JEFF KEMBER: So can
you talk a little bit

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:10.856
about the use case--
I have data in Europe,

00:16:10.856 --> 00:16:12.230
and I want to read
it in America,

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:13.950
and then I want to read
it in Singapore as well?

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:15.760
Can you talk a little bit
about how the buckets work?

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:17.700
DAVE ELLIOTT: Well,
really, it comes down to,

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:20.224
you know, a key
differentiator, as you

00:16:20.224 --> 00:16:22.140
talked about the
requirements for media assets

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:25.040
to be global in nature, right,
the person in India or Chicago

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:26.710
or Singapore needing
access to these.

00:16:26.710 --> 00:16:26.860
JEFF KEMBER: Right.

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:29.250
Because the work is often
done in multiple locations

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:30.240
and delivered
elsewhere, or globally.

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:31.190
DAVE ELLIOTT: Or the customer.

00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:33.130
Or the customer is in
multiple locations.

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:37.050
You may be web serving, you
may be serving out images,

00:16:37.050 --> 00:16:39.900
or you may be serving
out videos, right?

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:42.440
The customer might be
in various locations.

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:45.830
And because we have our own
network-- we own our network,

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:51.150
it's one of the
huge advantages--

00:16:51.150 --> 00:16:56.740
we've been pioneers in creating
ways to best optimize data

00:16:56.740 --> 00:17:00.130
movement across large distances.

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:02.600
So for a customer that does
have a global operation, that

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:04.530
wants global access
to their data,

00:17:04.530 --> 00:17:07.579
that's really one of
our key differentiators.

00:17:07.579 --> 00:17:10.069
In addition to the
network, we also

00:17:10.069 --> 00:17:13.400
are optimized for
reading very large files.

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:15.880
So we've optimized for
server side throughput,

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:20.040
meaning we simply are faster
in reading large files.

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:25.160
And this really comes from our
legacy in YouTube, in photos,

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:29.110
in Google Play, and that
means that your customers have

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:32.410
a better experience when
they use your media.

00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:33.960
The fourth reason
is our pricing.

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:36.510
It's both low and
simple to understand.

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:39.990
We have frequently declining
prices, as opposed to,

00:17:39.990 --> 00:17:42.600
say, again as a storage
guy, purchasing storage

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:45.470
hardware that has got to be
capitalized over three years.

00:17:45.470 --> 00:17:49.410
And on average, we're 13% to
15% lower than our closest cloud

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:50.570
competitor.

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:53.630
And you know, the feedback that
I get so much from customers

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:56.840
is it's just much
simpler to understand.

00:17:56.840 --> 00:18:00.120
The fifth major thing I'll call
out for Google Cloud Storage

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:02.640
differentiators is what I'm
just calling value added

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:05.320
services that can be
applied against your data

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:06.830
when it's in our cloud.

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:09.470
This goes back to your
accessibility comment

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:16.800
earlier, Jeff, where if
you can apply services

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:20.490
against the data, you can create
more value for your customers.

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:23.830
So massive scalability, global
network, large read file

00:18:23.830 --> 00:18:25.684
throughput, pricing,
and these value

00:18:25.684 --> 00:18:27.100
added services,
which I'll let you

00:18:27.100 --> 00:18:29.240
talk about in a couple minutes.

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:29.976
Make sense?

00:18:29.976 --> 00:18:30.850
JEFF KEMBER: It does.

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:31.683
DAVE ELLIOTT: Great.

00:18:31.683 --> 00:18:34.890
So I just briefly wanted to
introduce Google Cloud Storage,

00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:36.440
the actual service itself.

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:39.320
We've got three flavors--
Standard, Durable Reduced

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:41.810
Availability, and
Nearline Storage.

00:18:41.810 --> 00:18:43.940
They all come with 11
9s durability target,

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:47.050
and they primarily differ
in price and availability.

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:51.230
Standard Storage offers
99.9% availability SLA.

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:53.450
It's ideal for workloads
that require performance

00:18:53.450 --> 00:18:55.150
availability and durability.

00:18:55.150 --> 00:18:58.220
So it's sort of the standard,
maybe the premium one.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:01.170
Durable Reduced Availability
is exactly what it says.

00:19:01.170 --> 00:19:04.720
It's highly durable, and it has
a slightly reduced availability

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:06.380
SLA at 99%.

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:08.240
So it's ideal for
workloads that can accept

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:10.960
the occasional unavailability.

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:13.620
So for instance,
batch workloads.

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:16.460
Finally, Nearline Storage is
our newest storage offering.

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:20.590
And this is ideally designed
as a cool storage pool.

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:24.440
This is only a penny
a gig per month,

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:29.770
but it comes with a penny
read penalty per gig,

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:32.730
and it's ideal for
infrequently accessed media,

00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:37.030
such as backup files,
or even archive files.

00:19:37.030 --> 00:19:39.890
So with that, Jeff,
I'd love for you

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:41.640
to talk about that
fifth thing I mentioned

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:44.880
on the previous slide, which
was the value added services.

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:47.630
So we talked a lot about the
benefits of moving to an object

00:19:47.630 --> 00:19:49.338
store, the benefits
of moving to a cloud,

00:19:49.338 --> 00:19:51.900
and the benefits of moving
to Google's infrastructure.

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:55.220
But where I think a lot
of excitement is today

00:19:55.220 --> 00:19:59.980
is what can you do with the
data once it's in the cloud,

00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:01.520
once it's in our cloud.

00:20:01.520 --> 00:20:02.520
JEFF KEMBER: Absolutely.

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:04.270
One of the really cool
things about Google

00:20:04.270 --> 00:20:07.750
is the leadership, and the
lead that we have in machine

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:09.000
learning technology.

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:11.220
It is not an overly
exciting conversation

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:12.950
to say what does
a gig cost and--

00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:13.950
DAVE ELLIOTT: Hey, wait.

00:20:13.950 --> 00:20:14.902
Hold on a second.

00:20:14.902 --> 00:20:16.110
JEFF KEMBER: Well, it can be.

00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:17.700
But for me, what's
more compelling

00:20:17.700 --> 00:20:20.360
is things like the Cloud
Translate API that we have.

00:20:20.360 --> 00:20:23.660
The ability to have 80-plus
languages available.

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:26.586
You can put one language
in, auto language detection,

00:20:26.586 --> 00:20:27.960
you can get another
language out,

00:20:27.960 --> 00:20:31.640
and that works across both
text as well as speech.

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:33.790
So we have a Cloud Speech API.

00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:36.580
And that will allow you to
have both batch, as well

00:20:36.580 --> 00:20:37.600
as streaming, audio.

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:40.510
So as people are
familiar with, talking

00:20:40.510 --> 00:20:42.870
to your phone, that
can be in one language,

00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:44.580
we can do a translate
to text for that.

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:48.085
We can do a actual language
translation with that.

00:20:48.085 --> 00:20:51.270
And using the Cloud
Natural Language API,

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:54.610
we can then do a context
awareness of what was said,

00:20:54.610 --> 00:20:56.230
what was relevant
about that, and be

00:20:56.230 --> 00:20:58.140
able to deconstruct
the language,

00:20:58.140 --> 00:21:00.680
and get the sentiment of
what the user is looking for.

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:02.680
DAVE ELLIOTT: So this
sounds like a corner case.

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:04.090
It sounds like
something that may not

00:21:04.090 --> 00:21:05.131
apply to a lot of people.

00:21:05.131 --> 00:21:08.224
But the concept that
there's this service that's

00:21:08.224 --> 00:21:10.390
a really innovative service
that you're applying now

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:12.650
against your data, that you
otherwise couldn't have done,

00:21:12.650 --> 00:21:14.320
if it were just sitting
on a tape somewhere,

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:15.540
I think that's
really compelling.

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:15.970
JEFF KEMBER: Absolutely.

00:21:15.970 --> 00:21:16.470
Yeah.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.640
From an imagery
standpoint, I'd encourage

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:20.130
you to download Google Photos.

00:21:20.130 --> 00:21:20.990
It's free.

00:21:20.990 --> 00:21:22.890
It's available for
iOS and Android.

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:25.547
You can choose to compress your
images a little bit with JPEG,

00:21:25.547 --> 00:21:27.380
and there's no limit
on what you can upload,

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:28.463
if you choose that option.

00:21:28.463 --> 00:21:29.970
And the nice thing
about it is you

00:21:29.970 --> 00:21:32.250
can put thousands, and
many, many thousands, tens

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:33.910
of thousands, of images up.

00:21:33.910 --> 00:21:37.290
And the server on the back end
will do the metadata analysis

00:21:37.290 --> 00:21:37.790
for you.

00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:41.620
So if you type in Golden Gate
Bridge, or you type in cat,

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:44.680
or any of the search terms
for imagery that you may have,

00:21:44.680 --> 00:21:47.792
you'll be amazed at the
accuracy and the speed

00:21:47.792 --> 00:21:49.750
with which the imagery
can be presented to you.

00:21:49.750 --> 00:21:53.200
So the Vision API is fantastic.

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:55.350
You can feed it a
significant amount of imagery

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:59.050
and get back highly
relevant, scored metadata.

00:21:59.050 --> 00:22:02.020
And it comes to you with
a percentage of accuracy.

00:22:02.020 --> 00:22:04.110
It includes face detection.

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:07.150
It includes emotion.

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:12.080
You can decide if someone
is angry or sad or pensive.

00:22:12.080 --> 00:22:15.334
There's all sorts of
different complexities

00:22:15.334 --> 00:22:16.250
built in to the model.

00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:17.916
You can show it an
image of two kittens,

00:22:17.916 --> 00:22:20.230
and it will tell you what
kind of kittens they are.

00:22:20.230 --> 00:22:22.290
It's a really, really
compelling use case.

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:24.720
But what's interesting
to me is taking

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:27.990
a massive multi-petabye or
multi-hundred-petabye archive

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:30.670
that you may have, an
onlining that onto the cloud,

00:22:30.670 --> 00:22:33.010
and then running metadata
analysis against it

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:34.310
with the Vision API.

00:22:34.310 --> 00:22:36.570
That allows you to,
then, get at that data.

00:22:36.570 --> 00:22:38.260
It becomes cataloged.

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:39.200
It becomes useful.

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:40.467
You're able to monetize that.

00:22:40.467 --> 00:22:42.300
And you're able to
either resell it, or make

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:43.780
it available to
consumers, or make

00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:45.810
it available to your
employees in the company,

00:22:45.810 --> 00:22:47.490
depending on the type of data.

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:49.820
And that's just the current
roadmap of what we have.

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:52.960
There's more products coming
soon, backed by our machine

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:54.320
learning technology.

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:57.900
We also have BigQuery
and Big Data analytics.

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:00.840
Google is extremely adept at
handling large amounts of data

00:23:00.840 --> 00:23:04.860
with huge numbers of queries per
second, to be able to data mine

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:08.030
and come up with relevant
responses on massive data

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:09.320
sets, the queries you have.

00:23:09.320 --> 00:23:11.620
So if you have large data
that you want to work with,

00:23:11.620 --> 00:23:12.580
please use our tools.

00:23:12.580 --> 00:23:14.170
They are really fantastic.

00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:16.110
In addition, we also
offer transcoding

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:17.090
for media use cases.

00:23:17.090 --> 00:23:19.360
If you have images or
video that is in one format

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:21.450
